1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:05,921
At the Corner Cafe, Asha's report from
Chapter 25 keeps each sale as a pair, and

2
00:00:05,941 --> 00:00:07,700
each day as a dictionary.

3
00:00:07,733 --> 00:00:11,434
The rules, like the total, live in
separate functions.

4
00:00:11,454 --> 00:00:15,982
The data, and the rules that work on it,
live in different places.

5
00:00:16,587 --> 00:00:19,052
This is Code Quest, Chapter 26.

6
00:00:19,072 --> 00:00:21,617
Today, you build your own types.

7
00:00:21,667 --> 00:00:25,748
And an AI assistant will design a loyalty
card for Asha.

8
00:00:25,768 --> 00:00:28,605
It will sound sure about the free teas.

9
00:00:28,625 --> 00:00:30,656
Your test cases will decide.

10
00:00:31,253 --> 00:00:36,568
Here is today's mission: an order that
keeps its own items, and works out its own

11
00:00:36,588 --> 00:00:36,963
total.

12
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,586
By the end, you will know every line.

13
00:00:39,606 --> 00:00:41,769
Let's build it, piece by piece.

14
00:00:42,350 --> 00:00:44,700
Write a class, and explain self.

15
00:00:44,733 --> 00:00:48,843
Give an object a readable print form, and
write a data class.

16
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:53,031
And ask an AI to design a class, then test
the design.

17
00:00:53,617 --> 00:00:56,764
A class is a new type that you define
yourself.

18
00:00:56,784 --> 00:01:02,020
It bundles data and the functions that
work on it, like a menu item that knows

19
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,177
how to add tax to its own price.

20
00:01:04,200 --> 00:01:06,507
A shop has products and baskets.

21
00:01:06,527 --> 00:01:08,689
A game has players and levels.

22
00:01:08,709 --> 00:01:14,289
And AI assistants design code in classes,
so you need to judge their designs.

23
00:01:14,883 --> 00:01:16,256
A class is the plan.

24
00:01:16,276 --> 00:01:19,251
An object is one thing made from that
plan.

25
00:01:19,271 --> 00:01:22,873
Each object is also called an instance of
the class.

26
00:01:22,900 --> 00:01:26,043
Tea and cake are two objects from the same
plan.

27
00:01:26,063 --> 00:01:29,009
Each one has its own name, and its own
price.

28
00:01:29,620 --> 00:01:30,717
Class, the plan.

29
00:01:30,737 --> 00:01:33,790
Object, or instance, one thing made from
it.

30
00:01:33,833 --> 00:01:37,973
Attribute: a value stored in an object,
read with a dot.

31
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:41,190
Method: a function that belongs to the
class.

32
00:01:41,233 --> 00:01:44,458
And self: the object a method is working
on.

33
00:01:45,053 --> 00:01:47,602
Think of the cafe's printed order slip.

34
00:01:47,622 --> 00:01:49,446
The blank slip is the class.

35
00:01:49,466 --> 00:01:53,793
Each filled-in slip is an object, with its
own customer and items.

36
00:01:53,833 --> 00:01:56,226
The comparison stops at one point.

37
00:01:56,246 --> 00:01:59,135
A paper slip cannot add up its own total.

38
00:01:59,155 --> 00:02:01,193
An object can, with a method.

39
00:02:01,787 --> 00:02:02,364
Breather.

40
00:02:02,384 --> 00:02:05,617
One picture: a plan, and the things made
from it.

41
00:02:05,667 --> 00:02:08,166
Next, Asha writes her first class.

42
00:02:12,917 --> 00:02:13,497
Step 1.

43
00:02:13,517 --> 00:02:15,553
Line 2 starts the class.

44
00:02:15,573 --> 00:02:19,667
Class names use capital letters, like Menu
Item.

45
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:25,118
The special method init, written with two
underscores on each side, runs when you

46
00:02:25,138 --> 00:02:26,259
create an object.

47
00:02:26,279 --> 00:02:28,810
It stores the starting values on self.

48
00:02:28,833 --> 00:02:30,682
Line 8 creates an object.

49
00:02:30,702 --> 00:02:35,091
Python runs init with name set to Tea and
price set to 3.5.

50
00:02:35,111 --> 00:02:40,173
It passes the new object as self, so you
never write it in the call.

51
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:42,956
Line 9 creates a second object.

52
00:02:42,976 --> 00:02:46,806
Lines 10 and 11 read attributes with a
dot.

53
00:02:58,180 --> 00:02:59,012
Tea 3.5.

54
00:02:59,032 --> 00:03:02,420
Tea dot name, and tea dot price.

55
00:03:02,467 --> 00:03:07,254
And line 11 reads cake's own values: Cake
4.25.

56
00:03:07,850 --> 00:03:08,283
Step 2.

57
00:03:08,303 --> 00:03:11,393
A method is a function written inside the
class.

58
00:03:11,413 --> 00:03:13,660
Its first parameter is always self.

59
00:03:13,700 --> 00:03:19,109
Line 8 uses self dot price, the price of
whichever object called the method.

60
00:03:19,129 --> 00:03:22,610
It adds 10 percent tax, and rounds to two
places.

61
00:03:22,633 --> 00:03:28,343
Line 11 calls another method through self,
and shows it with two decimal places.

62
00:03:28,367 --> 00:03:32,171
Lines 15 and 16 call both methods on tea.

63
00:03:43,547 --> 00:03:45,467
3.85, then Tea: 3.85.

64
00:03:45,487 --> 00:03:50,917
In tea dot with tax, self is tea, so self
dot price is 3.5.

65
00:03:50,967 --> 00:03:55,056
And label adds the name in front.

66
00:03:55,653 --> 00:03:56,467
Quick check.

67
00:03:56,487 --> 00:03:58,897
In cake dot with tax, what is self?

68
00:04:10,280 --> 00:04:10,409
C.

69
00:04:10,429 --> 00:04:11,598
The object cake.

70
00:04:11,618 --> 00:04:15,167
Python passes the object before the dot as
self.

71
00:04:15,750 --> 00:04:16,248
Step 3.

72
00:04:16,268 --> 00:04:20,536
In Chapter 17, each function call had its
own local names.

73
00:04:20,556 --> 00:04:24,380
In the same way, each object has its own
attributes.

74
00:04:24,400 --> 00:04:29,640
Line 5 runs once for each new object, so
every order gets a new empty list.

75
00:04:29,667 --> 00:04:32,461
Lines 11 and 12 create two orders.

76
00:04:32,481 --> 00:04:36,601
Lina gets a tea and a cake, and Omar gets
a juice.

77
00:04:47,980 --> 00:04:50,284
Omar, and a list with only juice.

78
00:04:50,304 --> 00:04:53,100
Lina's tea and cake are in her own list.

79
00:04:53,133 --> 00:04:54,048
One more rule.

80
00:04:54,068 --> 00:04:59,189
A value written directly in the class
body, outside any method, is different.

81
00:04:59,209 --> 00:05:02,661
It belongs to the class, and every object
shares it.

82
00:05:02,681 --> 00:05:03,862
Remember that one.

83
00:05:04,453 --> 00:05:04,963
Breather.

84
00:05:04,983 --> 00:05:08,613
You can write a class, create objects, and
call their methods.

85
00:05:08,633 --> 00:05:11,366
Next, objects start working together.

86
00:05:16,117 --> 00:05:16,624
Step 4.

87
00:05:16,644 --> 00:05:19,334
An order can hold menu item objects.

88
00:05:19,354 --> 00:05:22,797
Add, on lines 13 and 14, puts one in the
list.

89
00:05:22,833 --> 00:05:26,291
Line 17 is a list comprehension from
Chapter 14.

90
00:05:26,311 --> 00:05:30,493
It collects the price of every item, and
sum adds them up.

91
00:05:30,533 --> 00:05:32,831
The order does not store its total.

92
00:05:32,851 --> 00:05:35,083
It works it out each time you ask.

93
00:05:35,133 --> 00:05:40,834
Lines 21 to 24 add a tea, print the total,
add a cake, and print it again.

94
00:05:52,213 --> 00:05:53,325
3.5, then 7.75.

95
00:05:53,345 --> 00:05:55,813
The total follows every new item.

96
00:05:55,833 --> 00:06:02,858
If the order stored its total as a number,
that number would be wrong after the next

97
00:06:02,878 --> 00:06:03,193
add.

98
00:06:03,213 --> 00:06:06,715
Keep that rule in mind for the AI section.

99
00:06:07,317 --> 00:06:07,772
Step 5.

100
00:06:07,792 --> 00:06:13,277
Printing your own object shows its type
and a memory address, a code that changes

101
00:06:13,297 --> 00:06:13,957
every run.

102
00:06:14,000 --> 00:06:19,731
The special method s t r, also written
with two underscores on each side, returns

103
00:06:19,751 --> 00:06:22,500
the text that print and f strings show.

104
00:06:22,533 --> 00:06:23,524
Asha runs it.

105
00:06:23,544 --> 00:06:29,508
Print shows Tea, with the price in
brackets, and the f string on line 13 uses

106
00:06:29,528 --> 00:06:30,596
the same text.

107
00:06:31,183 --> 00:06:31,656
Step 6.

108
00:06:31,676 --> 00:06:34,257
Many classes only store a few values.

109
00:06:34,277 --> 00:06:39,109
At data class is a decorator: a line above
the class that changes it.

110
00:06:39,129 --> 00:06:44,593
It writes init for you, a print form
called repr, and the double equals check.

111
00:06:44,633 --> 00:06:49,283
Lines 7 and 8 list the attributes, each
with a type after a colon.

112
00:06:49,333 --> 00:06:54,444
Double equals on two data class objects
compares their values, attribute by

113
00:06:54,464 --> 00:06:55,128
attribute.

114
00:07:06,480 --> 00:07:07,650
True, then False.

115
00:07:07,670 --> 00:07:10,730
Same name and price, then a different
price.

116
00:07:10,767 --> 00:07:16,540
And line 12 shows the print form the data
class wrote: the type, then each attribute

117
00:07:16,560 --> 00:07:17,506
and its value.

118
00:07:18,120 --> 00:07:23,976
Chapter 17 showed that a list as a default
value is a trap: every call shares one

119
00:07:23,996 --> 00:07:24,339
list.

120
00:07:24,359 --> 00:07:26,080
A data class refuses it.

121
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:31,626
Read the last line: mutable default, not
allowed, use default factory.

122
00:07:31,646 --> 00:07:33,844
Mutable means it can change.

123
00:07:34,453 --> 00:07:39,123
Field, with default factory list, makes a
new empty list for each object.

124
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:40,723
Lina's list has a tea.

125
00:07:40,743 --> 00:07:42,658
Omar's list is still empty.

126
00:07:42,678 --> 00:07:45,525
You have every piece of the mission now.

127
00:07:46,117 --> 00:07:47,718
Back to the mission.

128
00:07:47,738 --> 00:07:52,097
Lines 5 to 8 describe a menu item: a name
and a price.

129
00:07:52,133 --> 00:07:57,943
Lines 11 to 14 describe an order: a
customer, and its own list of items.

130
00:07:57,967 --> 00:07:59,958
Add puts an item in the list.

131
00:07:59,978 --> 00:08:02,109
Total works it out, every time.

132
00:08:02,720 --> 00:08:05,380
Line 23 creates Lina's order.

133
00:08:05,400 --> 00:08:10,000
Lines 24 to 26 add a tea, a cake, and
another tea.

134
00:08:10,033 --> 00:08:12,416
Line 27 prints the customer.

135
00:08:12,436 --> 00:08:19,193
Lines 28 and 29 print each item, lined up
with the format codes from Chapter 3.

136
00:08:19,233 --> 00:08:21,549
Line 30 prints the total.

137
00:08:32,913 --> 00:08:34,600
Total, 11.25.

138
00:08:34,620 --> 00:08:37,883
3.5, plus 4.25, plus 3.5.

139
00:08:37,933 --> 00:08:39,419
Mission complete!

140
00:08:39,439 --> 00:08:43,760
Next, Asha wants a loyalty card for her
regulars.

141
00:08:43,780 --> 00:08:48,809
She designs it with an AI assistant, one
stage at a time.

142
00:08:49,383 --> 00:08:55,402
This chapter's AI skill: ask an AI to
design a class, then review whether the

143
00:08:55,422 --> 00:08:56,343
design fits.

144
00:08:56,367 --> 00:08:58,999
Test every choice with real cases.

145
00:08:59,019 --> 00:09:02,743
And never paste private data: use made-up
names.

146
00:09:03,353 --> 00:09:04,425
Stage 1: plan.

147
00:09:04,445 --> 00:09:10,742
Asha describes the loyalty card: one stamp
per drink, and a free tea for every 10

148
00:09:10,762 --> 00:09:11,288
stamps.

149
00:09:11,308 --> 00:09:12,223
No code yet.

150
00:09:12,267 --> 00:09:15,468
The AI lists two attributes and four
methods.

151
00:09:15,488 --> 00:09:19,977
Print card and save card are separate
jobs, so Asha drops them.

152
00:09:19,997 --> 00:09:21,909
Her main program can print.

153
00:09:21,929 --> 00:09:24,916
One job for each method, as in Chapter 17.

154
00:09:25,520 --> 00:09:26,586
Stage 2: write.

155
00:09:26,606 --> 00:09:32,019
Asha writes the start of the class
herself, writes down her own prediction,

156
00:09:32,039 --> 00:09:35,930
Lina 2, and then asks the AI to predict
the last line.

157
00:09:35,967 --> 00:09:39,640
The AI says Lina 2 too, and the run
agrees.

158
00:09:40,253 --> 00:09:41,719
Stage 3: generate.

159
00:09:41,739 --> 00:09:43,204
Now the free teas.

160
00:09:43,224 --> 00:09:49,393
Asha asks the AI to add them, and to say
what a card shows after 25 stamps.

161
00:09:49,433 --> 00:09:53,838
The AI is sure: after 25 stamps, 2 free
teas.

162
00:09:54,453 --> 00:09:56,176
Look at the AI's class.

163
00:09:56,196 --> 00:09:58,903
Line 6 stores free teas as a number.

164
00:09:58,923 --> 00:10:02,539
Line 10 adds one when the stamps are
exactly 10.

165
00:10:03,153 --> 00:10:08,253
Asha runs it: 25 stamps, then she prints
the stamps and the free teas.

166
00:10:19,613 --> 00:10:19,851
No.

167
00:10:19,871 --> 00:10:22,515
25 stamps, but only 1 free tea.

168
00:10:22,535 --> 00:10:29,388
Line 10 of the AI's class counts only
stamp number 10, so stamp 20 never counts.

169
00:10:29,408 --> 00:10:31,450
And the AI sounded sure.

170
00:10:32,053 --> 00:10:33,157
Stage 4: test.

171
00:10:33,177 --> 00:10:37,573
Asha asks for test cases, each with an
expected result.

172
00:10:37,600 --> 00:10:41,123
The AI suggests 0, 9, 10 and 25 stamps.

173
00:10:41,143 --> 00:10:46,483
Asha adds one more: an old card that
starts with 12 stamps.

174
00:10:47,087 --> 00:10:50,555
She runs all five cases on the AI's class.

175
00:10:50,575 --> 00:10:55,040
Two fail: 25 stamps gives 1, and the old
card gives 0.

176
00:10:55,653 --> 00:10:59,743
Asha shows the failing cases, and asks for
the cause first.

177
00:10:59,767 --> 00:11:05,916
The AI explains: free teas is set to 0 in
init, so a card created with 12 stamps

178
00:11:05,936 --> 00:11:06,842
still has 0.

179
00:11:06,862 --> 00:11:10,852
The fix works it out from the stamps, with
a method.

180
00:11:10,872 --> 00:11:13,706
The same rule as the total in step 4.

181
00:11:14,320 --> 00:11:18,501
Lines 10 and 11 return the stamps, floor
divided by 10.

182
00:11:18,521 --> 00:11:21,633
Nothing is stored, so nothing goes stale.

183
00:11:22,253 --> 00:11:24,614
She runs all five cases again.

184
00:11:24,634 --> 00:11:27,788
Line 11 now calls free teas as a method.

185
00:11:27,808 --> 00:11:28,899
All five pass.

186
00:11:29,520 --> 00:11:30,601
Stage 5: debug.

187
00:11:30,621 --> 00:11:34,563
Asha adds a method, card line, and calls
it on line 5.

188
00:11:34,583 --> 00:11:35,370
Type error!

189
00:11:35,400 --> 00:11:41,691
Read the last line: card line takes 0
positional arguments, but 1 was given.

190
00:11:42,287 --> 00:11:48,157
She pastes the code and the full error,
and asks for the cause first.

191
00:11:48,200 --> 00:11:50,931
The AI explains: line 13 has no parameter.

192
00:11:50,951 --> 00:11:55,581
Python passes the card as the first
argument, so the method needs self.

193
00:11:56,187 --> 00:12:01,406
With self added, she runs all five cases
again, as a regression check.

194
00:12:01,426 --> 00:12:04,850
All pass, and line 14 prints Omar's card
line.

195
00:12:05,453 --> 00:12:07,021
Stage 6: improve.

196
00:12:07,041 --> 00:12:09,823
Asha asks for one improvement.

197
00:12:09,867 --> 00:12:14,927
The AI suggests a history of every drink
stamped, with a list at the top of the

198
00:12:14,947 --> 00:12:15,313
class.

199
00:12:15,920 --> 00:12:17,591
Here is the AI's idea.

200
00:12:17,611 --> 00:12:23,510
Line 3 creates the list in the class body,
and line 11 adds each drink to it.

201
00:12:24,120 --> 00:12:26,935
Asha tests the idea with two cards.

202
00:12:26,955 --> 00:12:29,852
Only Lina gets a stamp, for a latte.

203
00:12:29,872 --> 00:12:31,310
Omar gets nothing.

204
00:12:31,333 --> 00:12:35,580
Remember the rule from step 3, about a
value in the class body.

205
00:12:46,947 --> 00:12:48,308
Omar, with a latte!

206
00:12:48,328 --> 00:12:53,613
The list in the class body belongs to the
class, so every card shares it.

207
00:12:53,633 --> 00:12:55,285
The AI was wrong again.

208
00:12:55,887 --> 00:13:00,719
So Asha keeps the idea, but moves the list
into init, on line 6.

209
00:13:00,739 --> 00:13:03,222
Now each card makes its own list.

210
00:13:03,242 --> 00:13:08,454
Add stamp also takes a drink now, with Tea
as the default, on line 8.

211
00:13:09,053 --> 00:13:12,708
She writes her cases as pytest tests, from
Chapter 19.

212
00:13:12,728 --> 00:13:17,063
The helper on line 5, stamped, makes a
card and adds the stamps.

213
00:13:17,100 --> 00:13:23,841
Her five cases become five tests, and one
more test checks the history, on lines 32

214
00:13:23,861 --> 00:13:24,330
to 36.

215
00:13:24,367 --> 00:13:26,385
She runs every test again.

216
00:13:26,405 --> 00:13:29,129
That rerun is her regression check.

217
00:13:29,149 --> 00:13:30,776
Six dots: six passed.

218
00:13:31,387 --> 00:13:32,206
Quick check.

219
00:13:32,226 --> 00:13:33,954
A card stores its stamps.

220
00:13:33,974 --> 00:13:36,892
What is the best design for its free teas?

221
00:13:48,247 --> 00:13:48,352
B.

222
00:13:48,372 --> 00:13:51,164
A method that works them out from the
stamps.

223
00:13:51,184 --> 00:13:52,976
Then they can never go stale.

224
00:13:53,587 --> 00:13:54,238
Breather.

225
00:13:54,258 --> 00:13:59,607
The AI made two confident design mistakes,
and Asha's cases caught both.

226
00:13:59,627 --> 00:14:01,695
Next, four classic mistakes.

227
00:14:06,417 --> 00:14:09,027
Mistake 1 is the one Asha debugged.

228
00:14:09,047 --> 00:14:13,537
Line 7 has no self, so Python has nowhere
to put the object.

229
00:14:13,567 --> 00:14:17,310
Takes 0 positional arguments, but 1 was
given.

230
00:14:17,330 --> 00:14:19,028
That 1 is the object.

231
00:14:19,048 --> 00:14:21,319
The fix: def with tax, self.

232
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:22,706
Mistake 2.

233
00:14:22,726 --> 00:14:23,350
You try.

234
00:14:23,370 --> 00:14:26,412
Line 8 is meant to create a menu item.

235
00:14:26,432 --> 00:14:29,151
Look closely, and remember step 1.

236
00:14:40,513 --> 00:14:42,378
The brackets, and the values.

237
00:14:42,398 --> 00:14:47,513
Without them, tea is the class itself, the
plan, which has no price of its own.

238
00:14:47,533 --> 00:14:52,589
Type object, in the message, is the clue:
tea is a class, not an object.

239
00:14:53,187 --> 00:14:53,898
Mistake 3.

240
00:14:53,918 --> 00:14:59,380
Line 9 spells price wrongly, so the object
has no attribute with that name.

241
00:14:59,400 --> 00:15:04,132
At the end of the last line, Python even
suggests the right name.

242
00:15:04,753 --> 00:15:05,559
Mistake 4.

243
00:15:05,579 --> 00:15:08,203
This order class gives no error.

244
00:15:08,223 --> 00:15:13,324
Lina orders a tea on line 14, and line 15
prints Omar's items.

245
00:15:24,680 --> 00:15:25,132
Line 3.

246
00:15:25,152 --> 00:15:28,910
The list is in the class body, so every
order shares it.

247
00:15:28,930 --> 00:15:32,350
And there is no error at all, which makes
it worse.

248
00:15:32,400 --> 00:15:38,363
The fix: create the list in init, with
self dot items, or use field in a data

249
00:15:38,383 --> 00:15:38,829
class.

250
00:15:39,420 --> 00:15:40,340
Last breather.

251
00:15:40,360 --> 00:15:43,762
Two questions to think about on your own
come next.

252
00:15:48,483 --> 00:15:48,880
First.

253
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:52,910
Asha's cafe has tables, and each table has
several orders.

254
00:15:52,930 --> 00:15:54,369
Design a table class.

255
00:15:54,389 --> 00:15:59,023
Which attributes does it store, and which
value should be a method?

256
00:16:10,380 --> 00:16:14,179
Sketch it after the video, then try it in
the practice tasks.

257
00:16:14,787 --> 00:16:15,250
Second.

258
00:16:15,270 --> 00:16:18,980
Two data class objects with the same
values are equal.

259
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:24,299
Are two objects of a normal class, like
the menu item from step 1, equal too?

260
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:37,239
Predict, then test it.

261
00:16:37,817 --> 00:16:38,665
Let's recap.

262
00:16:38,685 --> 00:16:40,112
A class is the plan.

263
00:16:40,132 --> 00:16:42,717
An object is one thing made from it.

264
00:16:42,767 --> 00:16:47,257
Init stores the starting values on self,
and methods work on self.

265
00:16:47,300 --> 00:16:50,510
Attributes set in init belong to one
object.

266
00:16:50,533 --> 00:16:53,223
S t r gives a readable print form.

267
00:16:53,267 --> 00:16:57,667
A data class writes init, the print form
and double equals for you.

268
00:16:57,700 --> 00:17:01,314
And a value that depends on other
attributes belongs in a method.

269
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:03,014
One last check.

270
00:17:03,034 --> 00:17:04,946
Menu item is a data class.

271
00:17:04,966 --> 00:17:07,249
What does printing a cake show?

272
00:17:18,613 --> 00:17:18,747
A.

273
00:17:18,767 --> 00:17:23,969
The type, then each attribute and its
value, like line 12 in step 6.

274
00:17:24,587 --> 00:17:25,692
Quest complete!

275
00:17:25,712 --> 00:17:28,770
You earned 100 XP and the Modeller badge.

276
00:17:31,017 --> 00:17:32,638
Now it is your turn.

277
00:17:32,658 --> 00:17:37,397
Three warm-up tasks at 10 XP, and three
apply tasks at 20.

278
00:17:37,433 --> 00:17:40,072
And two stretch tasks, worth 40 each.

279
00:17:40,092 --> 00:17:44,959
In the last one, you fix an AI's order
class whose total goes stale.

280
00:17:45,550 --> 00:17:49,211
Today, Asha reviewed an AI's design before
she trusted it.

281
00:17:49,231 --> 00:17:53,210
But soon she will meet a whole program
written by someone else.

282
00:17:53,233 --> 00:17:59,094
In Chapter 27, you will read unfamiliar
code, and improve it safely, with tests

283
00:17:59,114 --> 00:18:00,583
and an AI assistant.

284
00:18:00,633 --> 00:18:02,406
Do the warm-up tasks now.

285
00:18:02,426 --> 00:18:04,413
They take about ten minutes.

286
00:18:04,433 --> 00:18:06,491
See you in Chapter 27.
